  "i": [
   {
    "id": "i1",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     8,
     20
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Tests for CharSet.getInstance() with \"odd\" range combinations where '^' appears mid-string as both a negation prefix and a literal range endpoint. Parsing rules (applied left-to-right, longest match first): \"^X-Y\" (4 chars, starts with ^, 3rd char is '-') -> negated range isNotIn(X, Y) \"X-Y\" (3 chars, 2nd char is '-') -> ordinary range isIn(X, Y) \"^X\" (2 chars, starts with ^) -> negated single isNot(X) \"X\" (1 char) -> single char is(X) Note: CharRange.isIn() / isNotIn() normalises start/end order, so isIn('a','^') == isIn('^','a') ('^' is ASCII 94, 'a' is 97)."
   },
   {
    "id": "i2",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     23,
     30
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "\"a-^c\" is parsed as two tokens: \"a-^\" -> ordinary range isIn('a', '^') (3-char rule, 2nd char is '-') \"c\" -> single char is('c') ASCII order: '^'(94) < '_'(95) < '`'(96) < 'a'(97), so the range covers '^', '_', '`', 'a'. 'b' is NOT in the range."
   },
   {
    "id": "i3",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     36,
     36
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "verify that the two expected CharRange objects were created"
   },
   {
    "id": "i4",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     42,
     42
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "'b' (98) lies above 'a' (97) and outside the range '^'(94)-'a'(97)"
   },
   {
    "id": "i5",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     45,
     45
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "'^'(94) is an endpoint of the range"
   },
   {
    "id": "i6",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     48,
     48
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "'_'(95) sits between '^'(94) and 'a'(97)"
   },
   {
    "id": "i7",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     51,
     51
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "'c' is the explicit single-char token"
   },
   {
    "id": "i8",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     55,
     62
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "\"^a-^c\" is parsed as two tokens: \"^a-^\" -> negated range isNotIn('a', '^') (4-char rule: starts with '^', 3rd char '-') \"c\" -> single char is('c') isNotIn('a','^') covers everything except '^'(94)...'a'(97). So '^' and '_' are excluded; 'b' (98) is above 'a' (97) and therefore included."
   },
   {
    "id": "i9",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     73,
     73
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "'b'(98) > 'a'(97), so it is outside the excluded range and is contained"
   },
   {
    "id": "i10",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     76,
     76
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "'^'(94) is inside the excluded range [^..a], so it is absent"
   },
   {
    "id": "i11",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     79,
     79
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "'_'(95) is inside the excluded range [^..a], so it is absent"
   },
   {
    "id": "i12",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     83,
     93
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "\"a- ^-- \" is parsed as two tokens: \"a- \" -> ordinary range isIn('a', ' ') (3-char rule; space is a valid boundary) \"^-- \" -> negated range isNotIn('-', ' ') (4-char rule: starts with '^', 3rd char '-') isIn('a',' ') covers ' '(32)...'a'(97) (order normalised). isNotIn('-',' ') excludes ' '(32)...'-'(45). Together these two ranges cover virtually the entire character space, because any character not in ' '...'a' is outside the negated range."
   },
   {
    "id": "i13",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
